#!/usr/bin/env node
// Derive ONE disposition per bound canon-overlay member.
//
//   node tools/derive-overlay-dispositions.mjs --scan     walk git history; write the producer-evidence input
//   node tools/derive-overlay-dispositions.mjs --write    derive and write the disposition ledger
//   node tools/derive-overlay-dispositions.mjs --check    re-derive; byte-for-byte diff
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
//
// program/canon-overlay-manifest.v1.json ENUMERATES 220 host-local durable
// evidence subjects. program/canon-overlay-bindings.v1.json BINDS each one with
// its content address, provenance, a narrowly-derived producer of record, and a
// default retention. For 194 of them the binding layer honestly reported
// UNATTRIBUTED: nothing in the BOUND GIT TREE and nothing in the estate names
// their evidence programme.
//
// An enumeration and a binding are an INDEX MECHANISM. They cannot own,
// classify, validate or certify the subjects they list, and CAS membership
// proves byte identity and retention ONLY — not provenance quality, not claim
// validity, not review, not ownership, not coverage. So this file does not ask
// the index who owns the index. It asks a different question against different
// evidence:
//
//   THE GIT HISTORY OF THE PRODUCER. Every one of these bytes was written by
//   something. For 185 of the 194 a tracked blob somewhere in this repository's
//   history literally names the member — by its exact path, by its basename, or
//   by the schemaVersion the member's own bytes declare — and git records what
//   became of that producer: rebound to a new locator, or deleted outright. The
//   chain is FOLLOWED rather than stopped at the first apparent deletion, so a
//   rename can never be read as the end of an identity. It is reproducible by
//   anyone with the repository and it is not the evidence citing itself.
//
// WHAT A DISPOSITION MAY BE
//
// Exactly one, from a closed set:
//
//   assigned_existing_owner     an existing accountable owner, named
//   assigned_new_successor      a named bounded successor record (proposed under the hold)
//   retained_nonclaim_evidence  an accountable custodian AND an explicit retention rule
//   retired                     a tombstone, an inbound-reference proof, and a successor rationale
//
// and `unattributed`, which is NOT a disposition. It is the honest refusal to
// invent one, it is reported member by member with the reason derivation failed,
// and tools/check-overlay-dispositions.mjs treats it as a withheld verification.
//
// EXPLICITLY NOT ATTRIBUTION, and refused by name in the bar: a wildcard owner,
// a baseline/index self-reference, directory-default ownership, and "preserved
// in CAS". If a derivation produces one of those for a member, that member stays
// unattributed.
//
// FAMILIES ARE EXECUTABLE RULES, NOT PROSE GROUPINGS. Each rule below enumerates
// its own members from a predicate over real bytes and real git facts, carries
// its owner and rationale, and exposes every candidate it REFUSED individually
// with the reason. A rule that cannot enumerate its own members is not a rule.
//
// DERIVED IS NOT DECIDED. Every entry carries `decision_source`, and the two
// values are mutually exclusive:
//
//   derived                 an executable predicate over real bytes and real git
//                           facts produced it. Nobody chose it.
//   explicit_owner_ruling   the OWNER chose it, at a named path and a named
//                           digest, because no derivation was available. It is
//                           read from _archive/attestations/owner-rulings.v1.json,
//                           applied ONE PATH AND ONE DIGEST AT A TIME, and it
//                           never passes through a family rule.
//
// That distinction is the whole point of the field. A derived ledger that
// quietly absorbs an owner decision launders a judgement into a derivation, and
// the next reader cannot tell which dispositions git could reproduce and which
// ones a person chose. tools/check-overlay-dispositions.mjs rejects a mislabel
// in BOTH directions: a ruled member reported as derived, and a member claiming
// a ruling that does not bind it at that exact path and digest.
import fs from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";
import process from "node:process";
